<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Masaniello</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1620">1620</a>–<a href="/years/1647">1647</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Tommaso Aniello</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Masaniello, born Tommaso Aniello, was a Neapolitan fisherman who led the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> against Spanish rule and a new tax on fruit in July 1647. For about ten days he dominated the city and negotiated with the viceroy, but he grew erratic and was murdered on 16 July 1647. The revolt continued and led to the short-lived Neapolitan Republic of 1647–1648.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/masaniello.jpg" alt="Masaniello" width="320" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Onofrio Palumbo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Onofrio_Palumbo_-_Masaniello.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tommaso Aniello was born in Vico Rotto al Mercato, one of the lanes around the market square of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, and was baptised on the day of his birth in the Church of Santa Caterina. He was long thought to be a native of Amalfi because his father was called Francesco, or Cicco, d'Amalfi. His family was humble but not poor: his father was a fisherman and shopkeeper, and Masaniello too worked as a fisherman and fishmonger. In 1641 he married the sixteen-year-old Bernardina Pisa.</p><p>Habsburg <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, burdened by several wars, laid a heavy tax burden on the Viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. Masaniello smuggled goods, chiefly for feudal nobles, and was imprisoned for evading taxes. According to tradition, his wife's eight days in prison for carrying flour into the city, and the ransom of one hundred crowns he paid, made him want to avenge the people. In prison he met the lawyer Marco Vitale, and he became a pupil of the elderly priest Giulio Genoino, who had once defended the people against the nobility.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The occasion of the revolt was a new tax on fruit. It began on 7 July 1647 with a riot at the city gates between fruit vendors and customs officers, and the customs office was burnt. The rioters forced their way into the palace of the viceroy, Rodrigo Ponce de León, Duke of Arcos, who took refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castel-nuovo">Castel Nuovo</a>. Masaniello gave audiences and administered justice from a wooden scaffold outside his house, and the crowd elected him captain-general.</p><p>On 13 July 1647, through the mediation of Cardinal Ascanio Filomarino, the Duke of Arcos and Masaniello signed a convention that pardoned the rebels and removed the more oppressive taxes. After that he behaved erratically, which contemporaries put down to strain, sudden fortune or poison. On 16 July he denounced his fellow citizens in the Church of the Carmine and was taken to a monastery, where grain merchants assassinated him. The next day the populace dug up his body and gave it a splendid funeral.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The revolt outlived him. A second revolution in August ended with Genoino's exile and the proclamation of a Neapolitan Republic under French protection, and the Spanish recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in April 1648. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a> used Masaniello's government against Robert Filmer, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-paine">Thomas Paine</a> named him as a warning in Common Sense. Daniel Auber's opera <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-muette-de-portici">La Muette de Portici</a> of 1828 is said to have sent its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> audience into the streets to begin the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgian-revolution">Belgian Revolution</a> of 1830.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Masaniello” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In July 1647 a new tax on fruit sparked a rising in Naples led by the fisherman <b>Tommaso Aniello</b>, known as Masaniello.</blockquote><a href="/state/neapolitan-republic-1647-1648">Neapolitan Republic (1647–1648)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.84,14.25&amp;year=1637" aria-label="Naples on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4899 706 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Masaniello"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5349 976h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5349 976h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.84,14.25&amp;year=1637">What was here in 1637? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
